Root-cause follow-up to B215. Deployed relay v0.12.0 git=a91bee6 on Railway (vigilant-laughter/c2c/production) goes unresponsive (HTTP 502; app upstream not answering) with NO OCaml exception/backtrace before each death - i.e. externally killed or wedged, not an app crash-with-error. Boots seen at 2026-07-15 09:27/09:44/11:46/12:47/13:25 UTC then ~2.5h silent while 502; railway ssh reports container 'not running / unexpected state (maybe serverless scaled to zero)'. Three hypotheses (see .collab/findings/2026-07-16T02-00-00Z-...-relay-down-crashloop.md): (1) OOM under the B210 multi-connect 429 storm + PoW-12 load; (2) Lwt event-loop deadlock/wedge (candidate: blocking Mutex.lock in relay.ml ~403/1502, or blocking I/O in a handler) - fits binds-8080-but-no-HTTP + TCP-healthcheck-passes-so-no-restart; (3) serverless 'sleep when idle' misconfig for a service that must stay resident, with failing/slow cold starts. To disambiguate: once up, SSH and read /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events (oom_kill) + memory.current/max; add a periodic relay memory/heap log line; verify Railway healthcheck is HTTP /health not TCP-only; check 'sleep when idle' setting. Fixes depend on which: raise mem limit / fix state growth; or fix the blocking-in-Lwt deadlock; or disable idle-sleep + HTTP healthcheck. Also deploy B210 broadly to cut storm load. Acceptance: relay stays resident and responsive under sustained multi-peer load; a killed/wedged relay is auto-restarted promptly; incident is diagnosable from logs.

UPDATE 2026-07-15T21:xx (master 107d6081, DEPLOYED): mitigation shipped, root cause STILL UNKNOWN — keeping OPEN.
- OOM RULED OUT (cgroup oom_kill=0; RSS ~28-64MB vs 8GB limit). Not serverless-sleep (no sleepApplication).
- B220 (railway.json restartPolicyType=ALWAYS) DEPLOYED → relay now self-heals (no more permanent outage after 5 retries); acceptance clause "auto-restarted promptly" MET.
- B219 instrumentation DEPLOYED + confirmed live: `[relay] heartbeat uptime=..s peers=.. gc_heap_words=.. gc_heap_kb=.. rss_kb=..` every 60s (baseline: peers=1001 incl-dead, rss_kb=64268 @ 60s), + Printexc backtraces, top-level Lwt exception guard (logs+re-raises), SIGTERM/SIGINT logging. Acceptance clause "diagnosable from logs" MET.
- REMAINING: the relay still dies silently every ~30min-2h under load (root cause NOT fixed). NEXT STEP: when it next dies, read railway logs for the FATAL/backtrace/signal line and the heartbeat trend (watch peers/rss climb) to identify the trigger, then fix. A death-signature log watcher is running. Note peers=1001 (incl dead) is high — investigate whether per-op lease iteration / dead-lease accumulation is a CPU/latency factor.

UPDATE 2026-07-15T23:xxZ (evidence-only investigation; root cause still open):
- Same deployment f75bd297 restarted: final heartbeat 22:08:50Z at uptime=4382s, peers=1047, heap~8MB, RSS=64.6MB; Railway remounted volume 22:09:24Z and relaunched at 22:09:30Z. No FATAL/backtrace or handled SIGTERM/SIGINT. Replacement exceeded prior lifetime (4922s) with /health 200, heap~8MB, RSS~44MB.
- OOM remains ruled out: cgroup oom=0, oom_kill=0, max=0, 8GB limit; current CPU/memory/IO pressure negligible.
- GC mismatch confirmed but not causal: production says gc disabled although CLI help advertises 300s. Live DB had 1131 leases, 1099 inside 24h TTL and 0 beyond the 12-month alias-release window that GC deletes, so enabling GC would not reduce current peer count.
- SQLite fd count ~109-122 is transient and far below 1,048,576 limit; one transient D-state process was observed but no IO-pressure/latency evidence ties it to restart.
- No speculative code change. Decisive next step: obtain Railway instance termination reason/exit status for f75bd297 at 22:09Z. Otherwise add a non-exec wrapper that persists child wait status, enable persistent core dumps, and have the external health watcher capture /proc/1/task/*/{stack,wchan,status}, cgroup pressure and fd counts after consecutive /health failures.